<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Hierapytna</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Hierapytna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">South Cretan port city that grew into the dominant power of eastern Crete in the Hellenistic age, destroying Praisos and contesting lands with Itanos.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hierapytna lay at the narrowest point of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, on the south coast at modern Ierapetra. Founded in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, it was a modest town in the Classical period and grew strong only in the Hellenistic age.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hierapytna made treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and with Hellenistic kings, supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and ships. It expanded aggressively, destroying <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/praisos">Praisos</a> c. 145 BCE and claiming the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diktaean-zeus">Diktaean Zeus</a> against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/itanos">Itanos</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> arbitrated the dispute in the late 2nd century BCE. The city was also accused of harbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hierapytna was among the last Cretan cities to fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Metellus</a> in 67 BCE. Rebuilt as a Roman port, it prospered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and baths, and survives as the town of Ierapetra.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hierapytna.jpg" alt="Hierapytna" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Steve Brabant, donation to Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corinthian_columns_ierapetra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hierapytna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ierapetra</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=hierapytna" aria-label="Hierapytna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5332 911 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hierapytna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5652 1167L5632 1166M5652 1167L5304 942M5652 1167L5661 1163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5632 1166h0M5304 942h0M5661 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5652 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5652 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=hierapytna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

